            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>Localization is more than just "translation"—it's not as simple as swapping languages. In Southeast Asia and Latin America, cultural differences, consumer behaviors, and platform rules vary significantly, making localization pitfalls more frequent than expected. This checklist covers 8 high-frequency mistakes to verify before launching any campaign.</p>
<h2>1. Language Localization: Don't Let Translation Kill Your Conversions</h2>
<h3>Common Mistakes</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Publishing raw machine translations</strong>: Relying on Google Translate or DeepL without local review</li>
<li><strong>Ignoring linguistic diversity</strong>: SEA markets include Indonesian, Vietnamese, Thai, and Filipino—each distinct</li>
<li><strong>Cultural slang mismatches</strong>: Latin American Spanish differs significantly from Spain Spanish; Brazil uses Portuguese</li>
</ul>
<h3>Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Ad copy reviewed by a native speaker (not just translated)</li>
<li>[ ] Small-language assets tested by 2-3 local users for clarity</li>
<li>[ ] Correct language pack matched to target country (e.g., Brazil ≠ Spanish-speaking)</li>
<li>[ ] Check emoji, abbreviations, and internet slang for local relevance</li>
</ul>
<h2>2. Visual Design: It's Not Just a Skin Swap</h2>
<h3>Common Mistakes</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keeping English UI layout unchanged</strong>: Text length, button colors, and icon styles may break locally</li>
<li><strong>Ignoring color taboos</strong>: Thailand has specific associations with yellow and white; LATAM users prefer vibrant palettes</li>
<li><strong>Using unfamiliar faces</strong>: Western models create distance with local audiences</li>
</ul>
<h3>Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] UI layout re-adapted for local character length (German, Thai text takes more space)</li>
<li>[ ] Color scheme tested locally or benchmarked against popular local apps</li>
<li>[ ] Landing pages feature local faces and familiar scenarios</li>
<li>[ ] Holiday-themed assets ready (Ramadan, local national holidays)</li>
</ul>
<h2>3. Targeting &amp; Bidding: Don't Copy-Paste Your Western Strategy</h2>
<h3>Common Mistakes</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Reusing European/American targeting</strong>: Mobile accounts for 80%+ in SEA and LATAM—desktop strategies underperform</li>
<li><strong>Bidding by Western benchmarks</strong>: CPC and CPM vary drastically across markets</li>
<li><strong>Ignoring network conditions</strong>: Indonesia and Philippines have high低端 Android usage; page load speed is critical</li>
</ul>
<h3>Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Device targeting set to Mobile Only or Mobile Primary</li>
<li>[ ] Bid ranges recalibrated against local historical CPM/CPC data</li>
<li>[ ] Landing page tested on 3G/4G network environments</li>
<li>[ ] Campaign scheduled for local peak hours (typically 7-11 PM local time)</li>
</ul>
<h2>4. Payments &amp; Compliance: Don't Wait for Account Bans</h2>
<h3>Common Mistakes</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Missing local payment methods</strong>: LATAM users prefer OXXO, PicPay; SEA users use GrabPay, Dana</li>
<li><strong>Non-compliant privacy policies</strong>: GDPR awareness has spread to parts of SEA; undeclared data usage leads to takedowns</li>
<li><strong>Prohibited reward mechanics</strong>: Some SEA platforms scrutinize "share to earn" schemes strictly</li>
</ul>
<h3>Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Payment gateway supports dominant local methods</li>
<li>[ ] Privacy policy available in local language</li>
<li>[ ] Ad copy avoids risky phrases like "guaranteed returns" or "limited time countdown"</li>
<li>[ ] Pre-check platform content policies in target market</li>
</ul>
<h2>5. Localization SOP: 3 Steps to a Qualified Launch</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Pre-Launch Review (3 Days Before)</h3>
<ol>
<li>Translation complete → Native semantic review → Cultural adaptation</li>
<li>Visual assets → Localized UI → Local user A/B test</li>
<li>Landing page → Network load test → Payment flow test</li>
</ol>
<h3>Step 2: Campaign Configuration (1 Day Before)</h3>
<ol>
<li>Confirm country/city targeting matches language pack</li>
<li>Set bids against local historical performance data</li>
<li>Schedule for local peak activity windows</li>
</ol>
<h3>Step 3: Post-Launch Monitoring (72 Hours After)</h3>
<ol>
<li>Watch for abnormal CTR or CVR fluctuations</li>
<li>Collect user comments/feedback for cultural mismatch signals</li>
<li>Pause underperforming ad sets immediately</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<p>There is no universal localization template, but there are repeatable checklists. Teams should固化 this checklist as a standard localization SOP and review campaign data weekly for continuous improvement.</p>
